In the context of this passage from "David Copperfield," the mention of the Black Prince serves to evoke a sense of history and continuity. The protagonist is reflecting on the passage of time and the changes that life brings. The reference to the Black Prince's armor hanging in the cathedral, while the bells toll, underscores the theme of time's relentless march and the contrast between the historical permanence of the armor and the fleeting nature of human life and experiences. This is further emphasized by the juxtaposition of the "age" of the bells and "youth" of Dora, the protagonist's love interest.

